  "strategy": "####\n1. Approach and rationale\nThe current solution uses a hybrid optimization approach (hill climbing and SLSQP) to minimize the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap integral. While it achieved a good result (0.3809), we aim for a better bound (lower than 0.3809). The key limitation is the method's dependence on gradient-based SLSQP and hill climbing, which can get stuck in local optima.\n\nOur approach uses a Covariance Matrix Adaptation Evolution Strategy (CMA-ES), a well-known evolutionary algorithm for expensive black-box optimization. CMA-ES automatically adapts the search distribution, handles constraints well (including bounds and sum constraints), and is more likely to find global optima than the current method. We will also exploit the problem's symmetry to reduce the effective search space. The population for CMA-ES will be conditioned through repair mechanisms, and we will use a hierarchical step: coarse exploration with population-based search followed by fine refinement with local search.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Representation: Symmetric step function h(x) defined on [0,2] with the property h(x) + h(2-x) = 1. The effective variables are the first n//2 samples (90 for n=180).\n- Variables: The individuals in the GA are parameter vectors in \u211d^90.\n- Objective: Minimize C5 = max_k np.correlate(h,1-h,mode=\"full\") * (2.0/n_points)\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i (via repair) and by construction of symmetry, the integral condition is satisfied.\n- Operations:\n  - Individuals: Represented as arrays of length 90.\n  - Fitness: C5 value, which is costly to compute.\n  - Mutation: CMA-ES strategy with Gaussian perturbations.\n  - Selection: Rank-based selection to advance individuals.\n  - Repair: Ensure all entries are in [0,1] via interpolation.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n- Step 1: Configuration Setup\n  Initialize CMA-ES parameters: population size \u03bc=20, offspring \u03c3=0.5, scaling factor \u03b7=0.1.\n- Step 2: Population Initialization\n  Generate random population with half-normal distribution for each gene, conditioned to [0,1].\n- Step 3: Main GA Loop (CMA-ES)\n  while time < budget_s:\n      a. Evaluate fitness for all individuals.\n      b. Update covariance matrix.\n      c. Generate new individuals via covariance-adapted Gaussian mutation.\n      d. Apply repair (bounds: interpolation for out-of-bounds).\n      e. Replace worst individuals with new ones.\n- Step 4: Local Refinement\n  Use SLSQP from scipy.optimize to refine the best individual with:\n      Objective: Smoothed version of C5 (log-sum-exp),\n      Constraints: Box bounds.\n- Step 5: Early Termination & Fallback\n  If stagnation for 10 generations, restart with perturbed population.\n  If elapsed time exceeds 90% of budget, return best found.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Primary variation: CMA-ES with dynamic covariance.\n- Repair factor: For interpolation, use exp(-10*|x-outlier|/mean_range)^0.5.\n- Mutation intensity: Adapt via expected improvement metric.\n- Estimation: 10^6 evaluations for CMA-ES, each rough (O(100) ops in Python).\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Dominant cost: 10^6 * O(10^3) = 10^9 operations (within 1100s).\n- Checks: Maintain NaN-free evaluations and feasible bounds.\n- Fallback: Return clipped/adjusted version of best.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Feasibility: 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 (via repair) and sum_constrained group-wise.\n- Re-evaluation: Compute exact C5 for final solution.\n- Sanity: Grid of 180 points, symmetric function.",
